' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548324 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548329 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548979 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [:db/cas 17592186548984 :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{16}{3},\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,-20,\\,80,\\,…$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=4f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1$" "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$\n\nII. $-5,\\,-20,\\,80,\\,…$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=4f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1$"] [:db/cas 17592186549016 :question/problem "![](images/78/78c5b7d059cac1d2ad94edf93a3d9317a62e5c1d1c88a4be29c49bbe9dac5f39.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=$" "![](images/78/78c5b7d059cac1d2ad94edf93a3d9317a62e5c1d1c88a4be29c49bbe9dac5f39.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549043 :question/problem "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ for which the polar curve $r=8$ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =$ \n{{field-1}}." "Compute the values of $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$ for which the polar curve $r=8$ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =$ \n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186549046 :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6},\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+{a}_{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=5f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{5},\\,n\\ge 1$" "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6},\\,…\n$$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+{a}_{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=5f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{5},\\,n\\ge 1$"] [:db/cas 17592186549924 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$760$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-40$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$760$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-40$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186549924 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=2{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=4{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i}.\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x,$ so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}76 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}760 & 760 \\\\ -40 & -40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=2{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=4{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i}.\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x,$ so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}76 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}760 & 760 \\\\ -40 & -40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549925 :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k$ has an inverse function given by $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186549949 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10}=${{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10}=\n$${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186549952 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549952 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 14 & -28 \\\\ 14 & 14 & -28\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 14 & -28 \\\\ 14 & 14 & -28\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549960 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549972 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549972 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 28 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 28 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549986 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 19\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 19\\end{aligned}],\n$$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186549991 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549991 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186550021 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|},\n$$ where ${D}_{x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186550022 :question/problem "Evaluate the polynomial $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}$ at $z=2.$" "Evaluate the polynomial $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ at $z=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186551233 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $R(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t=R(s-3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+3.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle R(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t=R(s-3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551235 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551240 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ee9e02184efd314371a99c6242540e5e2e6effc9e21d41a86648b433b8a6938.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/4e/4ee9e02184efd314371a99c6242540e5e2e6effc9e21d41a86648b433b8a6938.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551240 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d91a85ccbb948e070db738373ffc5032f4e91a0ba8e04cd14e79276b0e798f8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e0bffd04fb7b765d3c654f62b1958ab55a42f0d26cbc9f2b2e31f7d8e6f775.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d91a85ccbb948e070db738373ffc5032f4e91a0ba8e04cd14e79276b0e798f8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e0bffd04fb7b765d3c654f62b1958ab55a42f0d26cbc9f2b2e31f7d8e6f775.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186551246 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f94742a7f37fd7f6a1c789401a2e323fec80aca584774453cba03cf69f28ade.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/9f/9f94742a7f37fd7f6a1c789401a2e323fec80aca584774453cba03cf69f28ade.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551246 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4e8c62562ee1657e41bb36a21c901dc95935558641f724f934a4c58706f4a64.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/40/40b100dd4bc71b95f5ad99eb0cec8ed87133211bb371b22deccb851f79c695b3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4e8c62562ee1657e41bb36a21c901dc95935558641f724f934a4c58706f4a64.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/40/40b100dd4bc71b95f5ad99eb0cec8ed87133211bb371b22deccb851f79c695b3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186551247 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186551250 :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$" "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551260 :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5} & =\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3(x-\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 9 & 12 & 0 & 0 & -25 \\\\  & ↓ & 15 & 45 & 75 & 125 \\\\  & 9 & 27 & 45 & 75 & 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})} & =\\frac{1}{3}(9{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}+45x+75+\\frac{100}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\n$$" "We can calculate $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5} & =\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3(x-\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})},\n$$ we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 9 & 12 & 0 & 0 & -25 \\\\  & ↓ & 15 & 45 & 75 & 125 \\\\  & 9 & 27 & 45 & 75 & 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})} & =\\frac{1}{3}(9{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}+45x+75+\\frac{100}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551264 :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{BC}{12} \\\\ BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =9.456\\,12… \\\\  & ≈9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}\n$$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{BC}{12} \\\\ BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =9.456\\,12… \\\\  & ≈9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186551266 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551277 :question/worked-solution "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$" "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551286 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551296 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551306 :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551313 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186551313 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{3t} \\\\ y={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | $2.24$ |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | $4.48$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{3t} \\\\ y={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | $2.24$ |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | $4.48$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186551326 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186551327 :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551330 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-6t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $W(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t=W(s+6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-6.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-6t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle W(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t=W(s+6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551331 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186551331 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186551335 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{5}+1.$ Then $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}=\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+1},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{5}+1.$ Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}=\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+1},\n$$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186551342 :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =7 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(0.927\\,18…)} \\\\  & =7.549\\,74… \\\\  & ≈7.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}\n$$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =7 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(0.927\\,18…)} \\\\  & =7.549\\,74… \\\\  & ≈7.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186551349 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551349 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2(-1) & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2(-1) & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551357 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186551528 :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(- 8 x)\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(- 8 x)\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186551637 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(x+7)}{3{y}^{2}}.$ Assume $x+7>0.$" "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(x+7)}{3{y}^{2}}.\n$$ Assume $x+7>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552401 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e5cb856069061be9b877753ce6c373ee9191547fce8750e9c2a8b073b99ceb0.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V=$" "![](images/6e/6e5cb856069061be9b877753ce6c373ee9191547fce8750e9c2a8b073b99ceb0.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552405 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552419 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=-3.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552421 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [:db/cas 17592186552421 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552422 :question/problem "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552433 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $$\n\\displaystyle L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)\n$$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552433 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 34\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 34\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552448 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2f91e4ee1a90e1afb00d2f212e057235afe5c4e9089679da3af244c1d324676.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}12x\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" "![](images/f2/f2f91e4ee1a90e1afb00d2f212e057235afe5c4e9089679da3af244c1d324676.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}12x\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [:db/cas 17592186552450 :question/problem "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552453 :question/problem "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552454 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=15\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=15\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552459 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into $\\text{L1}.$" "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into $\\text{L1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552476 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552476 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552485 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186552489 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186552489 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-3 \\\\ -3+0+2 \\\\ -3+0+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-3 \\\\ -3+0+2 \\\\ -3+0+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552502 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186552502 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 \\\\ 1+0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 \\\\ 1+0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552510 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186552513 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=52\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=13\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-13.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-13I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]-13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-13)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]+13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=52\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=13\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-13.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-13I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]-13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-13)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]+13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552515 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =sin\\,(7\\pi (t+6)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t+42\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡42\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡42\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =sin\\,(7\\pi (t+6)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t+42\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡42\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡42\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552782 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(2,0)$ and $(-79,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(2,0)$ and $(-79,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552818 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{3} - 4$ traversed between the points $(-4,0)$ and $(23,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{3} - 4$ traversed between the points $(-4,0)$ and $(23,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552827 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{2} + 3$ traversed between the points $(5,1)$ and $(21,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{2} + 3$ traversed between the points $(5,1)$ and $(21,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552831 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552833 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{3} + 2$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(130,4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{3} + 2$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(130,4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552841 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(4,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(4,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186552845 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(3,0)$ and $(-78,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(3,0)$ and $(-78,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186553499 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186553503 :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{v}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)}^{3}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{v}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)}^{3}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553504 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $-2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{-2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$1-4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$1+4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $-2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{-2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$1-4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$1+4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186553505 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186553507 :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." "The lamina $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}\n$$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186553508 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=2x-2x=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,1)$ to $B(5,4)$ and  ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/18/18b1a5bdc11ca3c2b4b728cf8df3a3e1c4b6c2fbbe86549e9ad773367495c533.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+4j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2xy=-2(4t+1)(3t+1)=-24{t}^{2}-14t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((16{t}^{2}+8t+1)\\cdot (3)-(-24{t}^{2}-14t-2)\\cdot (4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,144{t}^{2}+80t+11\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[48{t}^{3}+40{t}^{2}+11t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(48+40+11) \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=99.\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=2x-2x=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,1)$ to $B(5,4)$ and  ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/18/18b1a5bdc11ca3c2b4b728cf8df3a3e1c4b6c2fbbe86549e9ad773367495c533.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+4j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2xy=-2(4t+1)(3t+1)=-24{t}^{2}-14t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((16{t}^{2}+8t+1)\\cdot (3)-(-24{t}^{2}-14t-2)\\cdot (4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,144{t}^{2}+80t+11\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[48{t}^{3}+40{t}^{2}+11t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(48+40+11) \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=99.\n$$"]]
